# libxshmfence -- a fence primitive in shared memory, and the package whose
# absence turns DRI3 off without failing anything.
#
# DRI3 lets an X11 client allocate its own buffers and pass them to the server
# as file descriptors; this library is how the two sides agree that a buffer is
# finished with. Xwayland's meson decides DRI3 like this (meson.build:339):
#
#     build_dri3 = dri3proto_dep.found() and xshmfence_dep.found() and ...
#
# under a `dri3` option defaulting to auto -- so a missing xshmfence yields an
# Xwayland with DRI3 quietly off. Nothing fails. X11 applications fall back to
# copying every frame through the server, which is slow rather than broken, and
# the symptom is "X11 apps feel sluggish under Wayland" months later. The
# xwayland recipe pins -Ddri3=true so that absence becomes a named configure
# error instead; this package is what makes that pin satisfiable.
#
# Not an LFS package, so it is pinned here rather than in versions.env.
SRC_URL=https://www.x.org/pub/individual/lib/libxshmfence-1.3.3.tar.xz
SRC_SHA256=d4a4df096aba96fea02c029ee3a44e11a47eb7f7213c1a729be83e85ec3fde10
SRC_DIR=libxshmfence-1.3.3

# BOTH FLAGS HERE REPLACE A DECISION configure WOULD OTHERWISE MAKE FROM
# WHATEVER THE BUILD CONTAINER HAPPENED TO CONTAIN.
#
# --enable-futex defaults to auto, and auto means "check for linux/futex.h".
# The header comes from linux-headers, which is packaged, so the answer here is
# yes -- but auto's failure mode is not a build error, it is a silent switch to
# the pthread implementation (configure.ac 78-88 selects the source files from
# it: xshmfence_futex.c or xshmfence_pthread.c). Both work; they are different
# libraries with the same name and the same soname. Naming it means a build
# image that lost linux-headers stops on a missing header rather than shipping
# the other one.
#
# --with-shared-memory-dir defaults to a search of /run/shm /dev/shm /var/tmp
# /tmp and BAKES THE FIRST ONE THAT EXISTS IN THE BUILD CONTAINER into the
# library as SHMDIR (configure.ac 152-170). That is a build-environment
# property compiled into a shipped artefact -- the same shape as
# wpa_supplicant's BINDIR. /dev/shm is what Duct actually mounts: duct-live's
# fstab and its rc both create and mount it, and /run/shm is mounted nowhere in
# this distribution. Left on auto, a container that happens to have /run/shm
# would produce a library whose fallback path does not exist on the target.
#
# It is a fallback path rather than the normal one -- glibc 2.42 has
# memfd_create and xshmfence_alloc.c prefers it (src/xshmfence_alloc.c 77-86) --
# but a fallback that cannot work is worth exactly as much as no fallback.
CONFIGURE_ARGS="--disable-static --enable-futex --with-shared-memory-dir=/dev/shm"
